  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-03-24T12:45:37.591Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Since VRChat doesn't want modded clients but seems to do absolutely nothing to detect them and ban those users, we've seen a flood of Udon-related malicious mods recently which can mess around with the overall experience that map creators have been added through Udon. \nWe can't expect every map creator to fully seal their creations against those clients, especially since the current system lacks any authentication of where a network event is coming from, making it way too easy for modded clients to abuse that system and most times impossible to secure the world against them. \nHowever, I've found out that it's extremely easy to detect some of those mods, and really don't understand why VRChat isn't doing the same. Do you want people to run around in worlds and ruin the fun for everyone? Have you ever tried staying in a public lobby without getting crashed by the same 5 people that do it every day?\nTl;dr, I'm willing to do it for you, please give me an API that I can use to self-report those users when my worlds detect one of their malicious mods. It's trivial to detect and even more easy to put booby traps everywhere which those hack0rs will fall into. \nAdditionally, a world should also be able to self-disconnect a client on demand. And we need network events that pass a parameter of the player that sent the event, authenticated from the PUN server, not the sender itself. Ideally, we would also get an API that would allow us to use private/public keys to authenticate that a synced string has been set by the user that has the corresponding private key.\nI'm aware that this is a neverending story and that there will be mods that can bypass this sooner or later, but we creators/users really just want to see that VRChat is doing at least some minimal effort against those malicious clients and are not letting them all get away with it. If there is some kind of fear that you actually ban users who run around messing with other people through malicious mods, they will reduce in numbers. It's usually not the \"new user\" that uses malicious mods, it's the \"trusted user\", which shows that there is nothing happening to ban them. Users speak openly about their mods and about crashing others in public lobbies as if they have absolutely nothing to fear, which unfortunately also seems to be the case as of now. I really hope this changes.",
